# Exercise 7: Exploring the `economics` Dataset

> **Question:** Review the definitions of the six variables in the `economics` dataset. Select two related variables and create a scatterplot with a title and complete axis labels.

## Variable Definitions

The `economics` dataset contains the following variables:

- `date`: Month when the data were collected.
- `pce`: Personal consumption expenditures, measured in billions of dollars.
- `pop`: Total population, measured in thousands.
- `psavert`: Personal savings rate.
- `uempmed`: Median duration of unemployment, measured in weeks.
- `unemploy`: Number of unemployed people, measured in thousands.

::: callout-note
I selected `unemploy` and `uempmed` because the number of unemployed people may be related to the typical length of time people remain unemployed.
:::

```{r}
#| label: exercise-7

# Open the help documentation
?economics

# Create the basic scatterplot
plot <- ggplot(
  data = economics,
  mapping = aes(x = unemploy, y = uempmed)
) +
  geom_point(color = "#7B2CBF", alpha = 0.65)

# Add the title and complete axis labels
plot +
  labs(
    title = "Unemployment Level and Duration",
    x = "Number of Unemployed People (Thousands)",
    y = "Median Duration of Unemployment (Weeks)"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

# Exercise 8: Creating a Chart with a Pipe

> **Question:** Recreate the previous chart using the pipe operator without saving the chart as an object. Add a figure label, caption, width, and aspect ratio.

::: callout-tip
The pipe operator `|>` sends the `economics` dataset directly into the `ggplot()` function.
:::

```{r}
#| label: fig-unemployment-duration
#| fig-cap: "Relationship between the number of unemployed people and the median duration of unemployment."
#| fig-width: 5
#| fig-asp: 0.6

economics |>
  ggplot(mapping = aes(x = unemploy, y = uempmed)) +
  geom_point(color = "#7B2CBF", alpha = 0.65) +
  labs(
    title = "Unemployment Level and Duration",
    x = "Number of Unemployed People (Thousands)",
    y = "Median Duration of Unemployment (Weeks)"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()
```

# Exercise 9: Interpretation of the Results

> **Question:** Use a callout block to describe the findings and cross-reference the figure created in Exercise 8.

## Checking the Relationship

```{r}
#| label: exercise-9-correlation

# Calculate the correlation to help confirm the relationship
cor(
  economics$unemploy,
  economics$uempmed,
  use = "complete.obs"
)
```

::: callout-important
## Finding

As shown in @fig-unemployment-duration, there appears to be a **positive relationship** between the number of unemployed people and the median duration of unemployment. When the number of unemployed people is higher, unemployment also tends to last longer. However, the variation among the points suggests that this relationship is not exact and may also be influenced by other economic conditions.
:::

